---
title: Special Bits
order: 2
description: SUID, SGID, sticky bit, and umask
tags: [permissions, suid, sgid, sticky, umask]
---

Special permission bits modify standard Unix access control. SUID and SGID affect execution identity. Sticky bit restricts deletion. umask sets default permissions for new files.

@text
SUID (Set User ID) — when set on an executable, it runs as the file owner instead of the calling user. Shown as 's' in the user execute position.

@code bash
# Set SUID
sudo chmod u+s /usr/bin/program
sudo chmod 4755 /usr/bin/program

# Remove SUID
sudo chmod u-s /usr/bin/program

# Common SUID binaries
ls -l /usr/bin/passwd         # -rwsr-xr-x (runs as root to edit /etc/shadow)
ls -l /usr/bin/sudo           # -rwsr-xr-x

# Find all SUID files on the system (security audit)
sudo find / -perm -4000 -type f -ls 2>/dev/null

# Note: SUID is ignored on scripts (only works on compiled binaries)
# Note: SUID on a non-executable file shows 'S' (capital) = no effect

@text
SGID (Set Group ID) — on executables, runs as the file's group. On directories, new files inherit the directory's group instead of the creator's primary group.

@code bash
# Set SGID on a file
sudo chmod g+s program
sudo chmod 2755 program

# Set SGID on a directory (most common use)
sudo chmod g+s /shared/projects/
sudo chmod 2775 /shared/projects/

# New files in an SGID directory inherit its group
sudo mkdir /shared/team
sudo chgrp developers /shared/team
sudo chmod 2775 /shared/team
# Now any file created in /shared/team belongs to group 'developers'

# Remove SGID
sudo chmod g-s directory/

# Find all SGID files
sudo find / -perm -2000 -type f -ls 2>/dev/null

# Find all SGID directories
find / -perm -2000 -type d -ls 2>/dev/null

@text
Sticky bit — on directories, only the file owner (or root) can delete or rename files within, even if others have write permission. Shown as 't' in the others execute position.

@code bash
# Set sticky bit
sudo chmod +t /shared/dir
sudo chmod 1777 /tmp

# Remove sticky bit
sudo chmod -t /shared/dir

# /tmp is the classic sticky bit directory
ls -ld /tmp                   # drwxrwxrwt

# Create a shared directory where everyone can write but only owners can delete
sudo mkdir /shared/dropbox
sudo chmod 1777 /shared/dropbox

@text
Combining special bits with numeric mode:

  Numeric prefix:
    4 = SUID
    2 = SGID
    1 = sticky bit

@code bash
chmod 4755 file     # SUID + rwxr-xr-x
chmod 2755 dir      # SGID + rwxr-xr-x
chmod 1777 dir      # sticky + rwxrwxrwx
chmod 6755 file     # SUID + SGID + rwxr-xr-x

# Permission display:
#   -rwsr-xr-x    SUID set, user has x
#   -rwSr-xr-x    SUID set, user lacks x (uppercase S = not effective)
#   -rwxr-sr-x    SGID set, group has x
#   drwxrwxrwt    sticky set, others have x
#   drwxrwxrwT    sticky set, others lack x

@text
umask — default permission mask for new files and directories.

@code bash
# Show current umask
umask              # numeric (e.g., 0022)
umask -S           # symbolic (e.g., u=rwx,g=rx,o=rx)

# How umask works:
#   New file default:      0666 (rw-rw-rw-)
#   New directory default: 0777 (rwxrwxrwx)
#   umask is subtracted:   result = default - umask
#
#   umask 0022: files=644, dirs=755
#   umask 0077: files=600, dirs=700
#   umask 0002: files=664, dirs=775

# Set umask for current session
umask 0022          # standard (owner rw, group/others r)
umask 0077          # restrictive (owner only)
umask 0002          # collaborative (group write)

# Set permanent umask in ~/.bashrc or /etc/profile
# umask 0022

# Verify: create a file and check permissions
umask 0022
touch testfile
ls -l testfile      # -rw-r--r-- (0644)
mkdir testdir
ls -ld testdir      # drwxr-xr-x (0755)
